# Line endings, once, for every platform.
#
# `text=auto` ALONE is the usual advice and is wrong here: it normalizes to LF
# in the repository and then checks out CRLF on Windows — which is a supported
# target, so contributors really are there. That lands CRLF in `scripts/*.sh`
# (a shell script git-bash then refuses) and in the ~25 tests that read this
# repository's own source files, as a diff nobody made. `eol=lf` pins the
# working copy too: one line ending, everywhere, in the repo and on disk.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except two third-party licence texts, which are kept byte-for-byte as their
# authors published them — both arrived with CRLF and must keep it.
frontend/public/fonts/LICENSE-IBM-Plex.txt -text
licenses/OFL-1.1-IBM-Plex.txt              -text

# Binary, said out loud. Git's own detection already gets these right; saying
# it means a webp whose first bytes happen to look like text can never be
# classified as text and mangled by the rule above.
*.woff2 binary
*.webp  binary

# Better diff hunk headers: git names the enclosing function or heading in the
# `@@` line instead of a bare line number. Only the two drivers git ships that
# match a language here — there is no built-in TypeScript one.
*.py       diff=python
*.md       diff=markdown

# GENERATED, so collapsed by default in GitHub's diff view: the SOURCE is the
# reviewable thing, and the generated copy is noise on top of it.
#   fieldHelp.ts  <- scripts/gen_field_help.py, from docs/training/fields/*.md
frontend/src/train/fieldHelp.ts linguist-generated=true
frontend/package-lock.json      linguist-generated=true

# The GOLDENS are deliberately NOT marked generated, though they are
# regenerated rather than edited. A changed golden is the most reviewable
# thing in a diff — it is the log, the manifest or the public API saying
# something different than it said before — and collapsing it by default is
# exactly the wrong way round. See CONTRIBUTING.md.
